<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Álmos, Duke of Croatia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1070–<a href="/years/1127">1127</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Prince Álmos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Álmos was a Hungarian prince, the son of King Géza I, who held the ducal third of the kingdom, the tercia pars regni, and was briefly king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. His repeated plots against his brother King Coloman led Coloman to abolish the duchy in 1107 and later to have Álmos and his son Béla blinded. Béla nevertheless became king in 1131.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/almos-duke-of-croatia.jpg" alt="Álmos, Duke of Croatia" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Márk Kálti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chronicon_Pictum_P105_K%C3%A1lm%C3%A1n_%C3%A9s_%C3%81lmos_kib%C3%A9k%C3%BCl%C3%A9se_D%C3%B6m%C3%B6s%C3%B6n.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Álmos was the younger of the two surviving sons of the future King Géza I, probably by his first wife, Sophia; the historians Gyula Kristó and Márta Font place the births of both brothers around 1070. When Géza died on 25 April 1077 his sons were minors, and the throne passed to their uncle, Ladislaus I, who unusually chose the elder brother, Coloman, for a career in the Church. Álmos held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> between 1084 and 1091 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> between 1091 and 1095. In spring 1095, falling gravely ill on a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, Ladislaus named Álmos his heir, and Coloman fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Ladislaus's death Coloman returned, and early in 1096 he was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekesfehervar">Székesfehérvár</a>. The Illuminated Chronicle says Álmos yielded the crown and received the ducatus, one third of the kingdom, with full rights. While Coloman campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> in 1097, Álmos gathered troops, but in 1098 the two armies met at Tiszavárkony on opposite banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> and their commanders refused to fight, forcing a peace.</p><p>When Coloman had his four-year-old son Stephen crowned in 1105, Álmos rebelled, sought help from Emperor Henry IV and then from his brother-in-law Boleslaw III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and took the fortress of Abaújvár. While he was on pilgrimage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, Coloman took over his duchy. Accused, falsely according to the Chronicle, of plotting the king's murder at the consecration of his monastery at Dömös, he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a>, and Henry V besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> on his behalf in September 1108 without success. After a further conspiracy Coloman had Álmos and his young son Béla blinded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Álmos lived in seclusion at Dömös until, fearing King Stephen II, he fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, probably about 1125. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos">John II Komnenos</a> settled him and his followers in a town in Macedonia, gave him the name Constantine and refused Stephen's demand for his expulsion; a war between the two realms followed from 1127 to 1129. Álmos died in exile on 1 September 1127. His son became king as Béla II in 1131, and his remains were brought back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1137.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prince Álmos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>King Coloman ended the arrangement, taking the duchy from his brother <b>Álmos</b> in 1107.</blockquote><a href="/state/tercia-pars-regni">Tercia pars regni</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1107" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Álmos, Duke of Croatia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1107">What was here in 1107? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
